# Docs: no surface catalogs

Human MDX explains how the part works and gives agent prompts. **Do not** maintain 1:1 inventories of ViraUI parts (token lists, “the N effects are…”, “these components are…”) unless the page’s job is literally a browser (e.g. color swatches).

## Why

Complete inventories become a second source of truth. When the DS adds or renames something, the docs drift. Skills, `@viraui/react/specs`, and theme sheets already own the live surface.

## Do

- Teach the **pattern** (gates, polarity, where to set, what to prefer)
- Name concrete tokens / props **inside examples** and short usage prose
- Point agents at skills / specs for the live set
- Soften defaults: “built-in brands pick a default per gate — check the theme”

## Do not

- Count the set (“four effects”, “eight layers”) when a count is not the teaching point
- Open with “ViraUI includes X, Y, Z…” as a checklist of what exists
- Add a section per item just to catalog the family (use examples or one “in practice” block instead)
- Promise completeness (“all `--effect-*` tokens are…”)

## Examples

| Avoid | Prefer |
| --- | --- |
| Four CSS custom properties — `--effect-vibrancy`, `--effect-corner-shape`, … | `--effect-*` custom properties decide whether whole treatments can run. |
| The effects are vibrancy, corner shape, holofoil, and shadows. | Show those names in a `:root` / JSX example; prose explains how gates work. |
| Built-in brands leave vibrancy, corner shape, and shadows on… | Built-in brands pick a default per gate — check the theme sheet. |
